Subject: Report a virtual destructor as virtual

`denote_virtualness` asks `self.kind()` whether the function is virtual, and
matches `FunctionKind::Method(MethodKind::Virtual { .. })`. A destructor never
takes that variant: `FunctionKind::from_cursor` gives a `virtual ~T()` its own
`MethodKind::VirtualDestructor`, so nothing was reported for one.

The half of the pair immediately above does count it - `MethodKind::is_pure_virtual`
matches `Virtual` and `VirtualDestructor` alike - which left the two answers
disagreeing about the same C++: `~T() = 0` was reported as `PureVirtual` and
`virtual ~T()` as nothing at all, the same as `~T()`. So a consumer reading the
callback could pick out a pure virtual destructor, and could not tell an
ordinary virtual destructor from a non-virtual one.

That distinction decides whether `delete` through a pointer to the class runs
the right destructor, which is the only thing making an abstract class
deletable at all; autocxx reads it to decide whether to generate the
`std::unique_ptr` and `std::vector` support which would do the deleting.

`denote_virtualness` and the `is_virtual` this corrects are both 09's, so this
is a fix to that patch rather than to anything vanilla bindgen has: it goes
upstream with the callback or not at all. It is kept separate because 09 is
already upstream-shaped and rebased as a unit, and because the two say
different things - 09 adds a callback, this one fixes what it answers.

--- a/codegen/mod.rs
+++ b/codegen/mod.rs
@@ -5104,9 +5104,17 @@
             return None;
         }
 
+        // A virtual destructor is virtual: `MethodKind` gives it a variant of
+        // its own rather than a `Virtual` with a flag, and matching only
+        // `Virtual` reported `virtual ~T()` as not virtual at all.
+        // `is_pure_virtual` above already counts both, so `~T() = 0` was
+        // reported and `virtual ~T()` was not.
         let is_virtual = matches!(
             self.kind(),
-            FunctionKind::Method(MethodKind::Virtual { .. })
+            FunctionKind::Method(
+                MethodKind::Virtual { .. } |
+                    MethodKind::VirtualDestructor { .. }
+            )
         );
 
         let is_dynamic_function = match self.kind() {
